<summary>  1.  <s>Revoked secret names cannot be reused</s> <code>✓ Resolved</code> <code>🐞 Bug</code> <code>≡ Correctness</code></summary>

<br/>

> <details open>
><summary>Description</summary>
><br/>
>
><pre>
><b><i>createSecret</i></b> permits insertion when a matching secret is revoked, but it creates a new row with
>the same tenant, namespace, environment, and key. The schema declares that tuple unique, so
>revoke-then-recreate fails at insertion and can also leave the version created immediately
>beforehand orphaned.
></pre>
></details>

> <details>
><summary>Code</summary>
><br/>
>
><code>[secfn/server/src/vault.ts[R272-275]](https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/179/files#diff-d9b90f14447c5925f609a065ceaab45ef2d92660649f31abbe12e934c1c427faR272-R275)</code>
>
>```diff
>+    const existing = await this.findSecretByKey(input.key, resolved);
>+    if (existing && !existing.revokedAt) {
>+      throw new SecFnValidationError("Secret already exists", { key: input.key });
>+    }
>```
></details>

> <details>
><summary>Evidence</summary>
><br/>
>
><pre>
>The duplicate guard ignores revoked records, then inserts the same lookup tuple as a new secret. The
>schema&#x27;s unique index includes no revoked-state predicate, and the encrypted version is created
>before the failing secret insert.
></pre>
>
> <code>[secfn/server/src/vault.ts[269-310]](https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/blob/99ea0ac4673fdde7fbb26a4f807a4040f203781d/secfn/server/src/vault.ts/#L269-L310)</code>
