# SFNS-4: Deliver Super Functions upstream capabilities and compatibility required by Rex

Source: https://linear.app/21n/issue/SFNS-4/deliver-super-functions-upstream-capabilities-and-compatibility

## Outcome

Deliver the reusable Super Functions capabilities and verified package set required by [REX-1](<https://linear.app/21n/issue/REX-1/build-rex-hosted-agent-portable-cli-and-shared-integration-platform>): a hosted personal/team agent with shared integrations, CLI/MCP access, BYOK, memory and durable automation.

Target consumer: SvelteKit + Tailwind + UIFn; Hono/Cloudflare Workers; DataFn on PostgreSQL through Hyperdrive. Keep generic provider/model/storage/runtime changes upstream and application-specific policy in Rex.

## Source baseline and evidence limits

Research examined:

* dev: 21nCo/super-functions at dac6abf87d57c36f010bc6360e60086ae0eeeca6
* next: 21nCo/super-functions.next at b21477fc9a32ae425c38a2de810b5a8705fc711f

Revalidate the current target branches before implementing. These are source-level findings, not completed release gates or proof of deployed failures. Required items below are capability gaps for the selected product/adapters; conditional items need evidence or an explicit adapter choice before becoming mandatory core changes.

## Required work

### 1. PlugFn — provider and action coverage

Expand first-party provider/action support to cover the finite Rex catalog: GitHub, Linear, Notion, ClickUp, Slack, Gmail, Google Drive/Docs/Sheets/Calendar, Jira, Microsoft 365 mail/calendar and OneDrive.

At the researched baseline, Google Drive/Docs/Sheets/Calendar and Jira coverage is missing; Gmail and Slack action sets are partial and Microsoft mail coverage needs extension. Do not equate a provider package or connection button with complete action support. Use INTEGRATIONS.md for exact action boundaries.

* Implement missing auth/read/write verticals, explicit scopes and account selection.
* Handle OAuth refresh/revocation, pagination, provider limits, webhook verification, polling/checkpoints where applicable, and provider-specific error mapping.
* Record per-provider/action readiness; unsupported features remain explicit.
* Keep connected-user outbound mail aligned with SendFn's outbound ownership where adopted rather than bypassing its governance.

Acceptance: every selected action passes positive/negative schema, scope, refresh/revocation, pagination and failure tests; write tests count actual effects; record separate provider sandbox journeys. Consumed by Rex phases 07–10 and 15–40.

### 2. PlugFn — richer reusable action contracts

Extend the action contract beyond input/output schemas and cacheable/idempotent flags to expose effect classification, required scopes, affected-resource hints, sensitive-field redaction, pagination and precise retry/idempotency semantics.

Unknown effects must remain unknown; metadata supports consumer policy but is not authorization. Version manifests and make schema/scope/effect changes detectable. Rex owns approval decisions and grants.

Evidence: [action types](<https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/blob/dac6abf87d57c36f010bc6360e60086ae0eeeca6/plugfn/core/src/types/action.ts>).

Acceptance: manifests roundtrip and validate; contract changes are detectable; logs omit sensitive fields; unknown effects cannot silently become safe reads; pagination/retry metadata agrees with provider behavior.

### 3. PlugFn — distributed limiter and execution coordination

ActionExecutor currently instantiates an isolate-local RateLimiter and workflow coordination includes local Maps. Provide injectable shared rate-limit and coordination contracts with a tested implementation suitable for multiple Workers isolates. Preserve and integrate existing durable job/claim/lease behavior; the entire system is not being characterized as in-memory.

* Define quota keys, lease ownership/expiry, recovery and fencing where needed.
* Coordinate retries, concurrent executions and process loss.
* Never claim exactly-once external writes when the provider cannot support it; surface uncertain dispatch and avoid automatic unsafe replay.

Evidence: [action executor](<https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/blob/dac6abf87d57c36f010bc6360e60086ae0eeeca6/plugfn/core/src/core/action-executor.ts>), plugfn/core/src/middleware/rate-limiter.ts and plugfn/core/src/core/workflow-engine.ts.

Acceptance: multi-isolate tests enforce shared quotas, recover abandoned work and prevent unsupported duplicate writes. Consumed by phases 01, 09 and 47–48.

### 4. LangFn — Gemini tools and native streaming

Current Google support declares no first-party tools and uses compatibility-mode streaming. Add:

* Tool definition mapping, tool-call IDs/arguments, tool results and multi-turn continuation.
* Native incremental streaming and normalized deltas.
* Correct system-message handling, cancellation and usage reporting.
* Explicit capability reporting and errors for unsupported operations; no silent downgrade.

Evidence: [compatibility matrix](<https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions.next/blob/b21477fc9a32ae425c38a2de810b5a8705fc711f/langfn/COMPATIBILITY.md>), langfn/typescript/src/models/google.ts.

Acceptance: streamed multi-turn tool roundtrips, fragmented arguments, cancellation, malformed responses and usage fixtures plus live Gemini verification. Preserve OpenAI/Anthropic compatibility. Rex owns the durable agent loop. Consumed by phases 42–44.

### 5. MemoryFn — storage, lifecycle and isolated retrieval

Complete reusable storage/lifecycle contracts before using MemoryFn for portable memory:

* Inject storage/Postgres clients instead of forcing a separately owned database connection; align shared dependencies.
* Resolve misleading storage factory behavior, including the SQLite factory's in-memory behavior at the baseline.
* Add update/delete, revisions/tombstones as needed, relationship cleanup and derived-index cleanup.
* Define explicit tenant/scope filtering that cannot broaden retrieval through optional defaults or tag overlap.
* Support retryable cleanup, stale-index handling and immediate denial after authoritative deletion.

Evidence: [storage contract](<https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions.next/blob/b21477fc9a32ae425c38a2de810b5a8705fc711f/memoryfn/typescript/src/storage/adapter.ts>), memoryfn/typescript/src/storage/pg/adapter.ts and memoryfn/typescript/src/index.ts.

Rex DataFn records remain authoritative for ownership, sharing and forgetting; MemoryFn provides derived retrieval, not Rex's membership policy.

Acceptance: update/forget cannot return stale memory; cross-workspace queries are denied; process loss and cleanup retries preserve deletion guarantees; injected Postgres works with the consumer runtime. Consumed by phases 45–46 and 51.

### 6. Coherent packages and runtime compatibility

Choose and publish/prepare a compatible pinned package set from dev and next. Their shared DB/HTTP/MCP dependencies and package surfaces differ; do not mix branch snapshots blindly.

* Record source SHAs, package versions and artifact integrity.
* Resolve dependency/export drift and validate installed package artifacts, not only monorepo source imports.
* Prove Workers/workerd compatibility, Hono integration, Svelte SSR/hydration, and real PostgreSQL through Hyperdrive.
* Validate migrations, transaction rollback, connection lifecycle and absence of unintended Node-only imports in Worker entrypoints.
* Document the supported matrix and upgrade/migration implications.

Acceptance: isolated packed-consumer tests and staging canaries pass with reproducible provenance. Consumed by phases 00–02 and 55–56.

## Recommended reusable improvements / conditional work

### CliFn

Provide injectable credential storage, OS keychain integration or a safe consumer adapter, atomic writes and explicit app-specific config/service namespaces. Current Conduct-oriented plain INI storage with restrictive permissions is not an OS keychain. Add retry controls that do not replay uncertain writes. Rex can provide these adapters without requiring a core rewrite.

Acceptance: secure storage/fallback, concurrent writes, redaction and idempotency behavior tested. Generic distribution helpers should preserve conflicting executables. Rex package rex-agent exposes primary rex and fallback rex-agent; naming and host setup remain the consumer's responsibility. Phases 06, 11, 53.

### AuthFn and McpFn

Use existing human identity/native handoff and hosted authorization hooks. A generic device authorization plugin is useful but optional; native handoff alone is not a complete device grant/token authority. Reconcile documented versus implemented handoff expiry defaults if still divergent.

Validate Worker-safe package entrypoints, token/consent hook boundaries, remote client lifecycle, cancellation, reconnection and hosted/stdio compatibility. Fix concrete issues found by canaries. Rex owns memberships, client grants, device consent and revocation policy.

Acceptance: device expiry/abuse/revocation tests and MCP conformance against named supported hosts. Phases 01, 03–06, 12–14.

### DataFn, UIFn, SecFn and FileFn

No mandatory redesign was established. Run packaged consumer gates for safe data exposure, privileged operation boundaries, tenant isolation, secret rotation/redaction, blob authorization, Svelte SSR/hydration and accessibility. Fix generic defects revealed by these gates and document consumer responsibilities. Phases 01–02, 41, 49, 51–52.

### SendFn and FlowFn

If the outbound mail boundary requires it, add reusable Gmail/Microsoft connected-account SendFn adapters with refresh, approval context, retries and delivery receipts. Confirm ownership with PlugFn rather than implementing inconsistent duplicate outbound paths.

A Cloudflare-backed FlowFn adapter is optional because Rex can use Workflows/Queues directly. Do not treat local timer scheduling as durable hosted execution. If an adapter is adopted, verify crash/replay, deduplication, schedule cancellation, webhook authenticity and checkpoint recovery. Phases 26, 38, 47–48.

## Ownership and delivery order

1. Revalidate snapshots and choose the package/runtime baseline.
2. Land shared compatibility fixes and PlugFn execution/action contracts.
3. Implement provider verticals against those contracts.
4. Complete LangFn and MemoryFn work before their consuming phases.
5. Resolve conditional adapters based on canary evidence; record rationale for any item satisfied by an existing extension point.
6. Publish exact upstream refs and consumer migration guidance; Rex pins only the validated artifacts.

Team/workspace policy, approval UX, client grants, durable agent state, device registry, automation authority and product billing belong in <issue id="cbcacc4d-546f-4712-8aec-6842b3fccb33" href="https://linear.app/21n/issue/REX-1/build-rex-hosted-agent-portable-cli-and-shared-integration-platform">REX-1</issue>. This issue should not turn every product feature into a new core function API.

## Definition of done

* Required capabilities above are implemented with positive and negative contract tests.
* Conditional items have an evidence-backed resolution: upstream fix, documented supported adapter, or demonstrated no change needed.
* Full selected provider matrix has truthful readiness and separately recorded live sandbox evidence.
* Multi-isolate, credential lifecycle, memory deletion/isolation and model tool-loop failure tests pass.
* Exact compatible release artifacts, source references, migration notes and Rex phase dependencies are documented.
* Local tests, hosted staging and live provider/model checks are reported separately; no source-only claim of production readiness.

